<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joanna la Beltraneja</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1462">1462</a>–<a href="/years/1530">1530</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-trastamara">House of Trastámara</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joanna la Beltraneja was the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-castile">Henry IV of Castile</a>, whose enemies claimed she was the child of the courtier Beltrán de la Cueva. Backed by her uncle Afonso V of Portugal, she contested the throne with Isabella I in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-castilian-succession">War of the Castilian Succession</a> of 1475–1479. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-alcacovas">Treaty of Alcáçovas</a> she lived in Portugal until her death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joanna-la-beltraneja.jpg" alt="Joanna la Beltraneja" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: António de Holanda · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joana,_a_Excelente_Senhora_-_The_Portuguese_Genealogy_(Genealogia_dos_Reis_de_Portugal).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joanna was born on 28 February 1462 at the Royal Alcazar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-castile">Henry IV of Castile</a> and Joan of Portugal, whom he had married in 1455. On 9 May 1462 she was proclaimed heir to the throne and created Princess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>. In 1464 a faction of nobles led by Juan Pacheco, Alfonso Carrillo de Acuña and Pedro Girón, angered by the royal favourite Beltrán de la Cueva's appointment as master of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-santiago">Order of Santiago</a>, circulated rumours that Joanna was his child. Her opponents began to call her la Beltraneja, a mocking reference to her supposed illegitimacy, and later also argued that her parents had lacked the papal dispensation needed for their marriage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Civil war broke out in 1465, when the rebels deposed Henry in effigy and crowned his half-brother Alfonso. After Alfonso died in 1468, Henry and his half-sister Isabella agreed the Treaty of the Bulls of Guisando in September 1468, naming Isabella heir instead of Joanna. The Mendoza family rallied to Joanna's cause. Isabella married Ferdinand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> in 1469 without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s consent, and in October 1470 Joanna was again proclaimed legitimate heir and betrothed to Charles, Duke of Berry, who in the end withdrew. She was then promised to her uncle, King Afonso V of Portugal.</p><p>When Henry died in 1474, some nobles recognised Joanna as queen and others preferred Isabella, beginning the four-year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-castilian-succession">War of the Castilian Succession</a>. On 10 May 1475 Afonso invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, and he married Joanna at Plasencia 15 days later. At the Battle of Toro on 1 March 1476 the outcome was indecisive, but Ferdinand sent word of a great victory to the cities of Castile, and the prestige of Joanna and Afonso dissolved. The Portuguese returned to their kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1478 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> annulled the marriage of Joanna and Afonso on grounds of consanguinity, and she renounced the title of queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-alcacovas">Treaty of Alcáçovas</a> of 1479 she was offered a choice between a convent and marriage to Isabella's one-year-old son John; she chose the Convent of Santa Clara in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a>. Ferdinand and Isabella lobbied the Holy See to ensure that she kept her vows. In 1522 she relinquished her title and offered the crown to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-of-portugal">John III of Portugal</a>. She signed her letters La Reina until she died, and was known in Portugal as the Excellent Lady. She died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> on 12 April 1530.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joanna la Beltraneja” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.7&amp;year=1506" aria-label="Madrid on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Joanna la Beltraneja"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4759 4110h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4759 4110h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.7&amp;year=1506">What was here in 1506? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
